# Copy to `.env` for local use. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so it
# must never contain a real value — only names and explanations.
#
# Which of these belong where:
#
#   Netlify  → Site configuration → Environment variables
#   CI       → repository secrets
#   Local    → your shell

# ── Required ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Nothing. Every dependency resolves from the public npm registry or from this
# workspace, so builds need no credentials at all — not locally, not in CI,
# not on Netlify.

# ── Optional ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which ContentAdapter / AuthProvider each app injects at server start. Both
# apps/shell and apps/cms read the same two variables. Both default to the
# backend-free option, so a fresh clone runs with no setup at all. Set either
# to "supabase" once you have a local (`supabase start`) or hosted project —
# see supabase/README.md.
# CONTENT_ADAPTER=memory
# AUTH_PROVIDER=mock

# apps/cms's role and route-bundle editors follow AUTH_PROVIDER rather than a
# variable of their own — see apps/cms/src/server/adapters.ts's own note on
# why a config-bundle write is only ever meaningful against the same real
# session AUTH_PROVIDER=supabase already provides.

# Required when CONTENT_ADAPTER or AUTH_PROVIDER is "supabase". Note the split
# carefully:
#
#   SUPABASE_URL              safe to expose; identifies the project
#   SUPABASE_ANON_KEY         safe to expose; RLS is what protects the data
#   SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY SERVER ONLY — bypasses RLS entirely
#
# Vite inlines any variable prefixed `VITE_` into the client bundle, where it
# is readable by anyone. The service role key must never carry that prefix,
# and must never be read outside a server function. Nothing in either app
# reads it at all — every Supabase-backed adapter uses only the anon key,
# since every table it touches is either public-read RLS (content reads),
# protected by the caller's own session (auth, config bundle writes, content
# writes). It is listed here for the contract suites
# (`content-adapter-supabase`, `auth-supabase`, `config-bundle-supabase`),
# which use it to seed and tear down fixtures with `supabase.auth.admin` —
# see each package's `src/contract.test.ts` or `src/live.test.ts`.
# SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=
